  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrently, broad troughing is deepening across the eastern U.S. with \nan embedded shortwave over the western Great Lakes. At the surface, \nCanadian high pressure is progressing south from upper Ontario with \nthe recent surface low off the east coast. This has put the region \nin a northwesterly flow pattern with CAA dropping the freezing level \nto near 4,000 feet ASL confirmed by high elevation obs. Morning \nshowers will remain as snow for places this high with a lull in \nactivity for the first half of the day. By mid-afternoon, the \nreinforcing shortwave will arrive from the north with moisture fetch \nfrom Lake Michigan supporting another increase in showers. By this \ntime, freezing levels are expected to drop to near 3,500 feet and \nthen to 3,000 feet and lower after sunset. With the continued \nmoisture and persistent 20 to 25 kts of northwesterly flow, snow \nwill be focused along the higher elevations with potential for \nsprinkles and flurries in the lower elevations. Between about 1 AM \nand 4 AM EST, moisture above 850mb will become a lot more limited, \ninhibiting additional high elevation snowfall. As the Canadian high \nbuilds in from the west, moisture will shift towards the northeast \nSaturday morning before area-wide clearing. All in all, the latest \nhigh-res model guidance continues to suggest similar snow \naccumulations focused near and above 3,000 feet AGL with some \nlighter amounts possible for lower elevation places. \n\nAs the Canadian high settles over the region, strong subsidence will \nmake for notably cold temperatures falling into the 20s by Sunday \nmorning. By the day on Sunday, high pressure remains in place with \ntroughing continuing to shift eastward, leading to a recovery of \nhighs into the 50s as opposed to mainly 40s on Saturday. High \npressure will remain in place through next week with gradual height \nrises continuing the warming trend. As 500mb heights rise above \nnormal to 5,750m by Tuesday, daytime highs will likely reach into \nthe 60s for lower elevation places, followed by higher values \nWednesday and Thursday. Continued subsidence will still make for \ncool mornings, in addition to potentially low RH's in the\nafternoons.",
